# Accessibility

All interfaces must be accessible by default — not as an afterthought.

## Semantic HTML

- Use the correct HTML element for its intended purpose.
- Never use `<div>` or `<span>` for interactive elements — use `<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, etc.
- Use landmark elements (`<main>`, `<nav>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`) to structure pages.

```tsx
// bad
<div onClick={handleClick}>Save</div>

// good
<button onClick={handleClick}>Save</button>
```

## Keyboard Navigation

- All interactive elements must be reachable and operable via keyboard alone.
- Maintain a logical tab order — do not use `tabIndex` values greater than `0`.
- Modal dialogs and dropdowns must trap focus while open and restore focus on close.

## Focus States

- Never remove focus outlines with `outline: none` without providing a visible custom replacement.
- Focus styles must be clearly visible against the dark background — use the neon green accent.

## Accessible Labels

- Every `<input>`, `<select>` and `<textarea>` must have an associated `<label>`.
- Icon-only buttons must have `aria-label` describing their action.
- Images that convey meaning must have descriptive `alt` text. Decorative images use `alt=""`.

```tsx
// bad
<button><SearchIcon /></button>

// good
<button aria-label="Search transactions"><SearchIcon /></button>
```

## Color Contrast

- Text contrast must meet WCAG AA minimum: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text.
- Never rely on color alone to convey information — always pair color with a label, icon or pattern.
